    power: "Striking every kind of plant with it, he learned whether each was warming or cooling and whether it was poisonous.",
    firstRecord: "Gan Bao, In Search of the Supernatural",
    summary: "The red whip with which Shennong is said to have struck every plant to tell medicine from poison.",
    story: `In Search of the Supernatural, compiled by Gan Bao of the Eastern Jin, begins with Shennong. Shennong struck every kind of plant with a red whip and learned whether each was mild or poisonous, cold or warm, and he examined their smells and tastes. Because he then had people plant all the grains, the world called him Shennong, the Divine Farmer.

Shennong is one of the Three Sovereigns of Chinese legend, said to have first taught agriculture and medicine. The Huainanzi says he tasted every plant himself, encountering seventy poisons in a single day.

China's first book of materia medica, the Divine Farmer's Classic of Materia Medica, borrowed his name. In Japan and Korea too Shennong was revered as the ancestor of medicine, and pharmacies and physicians sometimes hung his picture.`,
