    firstRecord: "Kyokutei Bakin, The Eight Dog Chronicles (Nansō Satomi Hakkenden)",
    summary: "Eight jewels scattered from Princess Fuse's prayer beads. A character for a virtue appeared on each, linking the eight warriors who carried them.",
    story: `In Nansō Satomi Hakkenden, Satomi Yoshizane, lord of Awa, besieged in his castle, says half in jest that he will give his daughter Princess Fuse to whoever brings him the head of the enemy general. His dog Yatsufusa really does bring back the enemy general's head in its jaws, and Princess Fuse, bound to keep the promise, goes with the dog deep into the mountains.

In the mountains Princess Fuse became pregnant through the dog's spirit, and in shame she took her own life. At that moment the eight large beads of the rosary she carried scattered into the sky. On each bead appeared one of the characters for the eight virtues: benevolence, righteousness, courtesy, wisdom, loyalty, faith, filial piety and brotherly duty.

The beads came to rest, one each, in eight children born in different places. These children, whose names all contained the character for 'dog,' grew up, recognized one another and gathered, becoming the Eight Dog Warriors who fought for the Satomi clan.`,
    imageAlt: "A two-panel kabuki actor print made in Japan in 1834: Arashi Rikan II as Inuzuka Shino and Nakamura Utaemon III as Inukai Genpachi in battle. Two warriors with swords grapple on a tiled roof, with the roles and story written in the panels above.",
